  "source": "issue #385",
  "statement": "Complete saves use per-file atomic writes for chunks, plants and energy before the metadata phase in both region modes. Required state-writer failures retain the previous metadata and pending dirty chunks. Regions-disabled incremental saves preserve devel file layout and metadata bytes, including plant-only containers and unchanged server shutdowns. Explicit full snapshots provide complete save-as exports and retain canonical dirty flags and save stamps, including destinations with trailing separators. Plant, region and manifest writers stage outside the strict region scan and reclaim their abandoned staging before replacement; plant cleanup also runs for an empty roster. Client preset and settings writers check durable atomic publication before reporting success or removing a renamed source. Registered regressions cover writer failures, malformed presets, interrupted replacement, retries and partial chunk writes. Native execution remains required. The shared region staging calculation preserves Windows UNC roots and the original save-root prefix, relocates only an original chunks/region suffix, and keeps other spellings adjacent; path diagnostics require no filesystem access, and native Windows durable-save qualification remains required. A POSIX regression covers a region symlink outside the save root with read-only target ancestors, abandoned staging, interrupted replacement and retry; native execution remains required. Bare relative container destinations reclaim abandoned staging in the current directory. All persistence writers serialize within the process through reclamation and publication, including region merges and empty-layer removal. Shared writes, metadata and empty energy and ledger saves reclaim abandoned staging while holding that ownership. Barrier-based overlapping region reclamation and shared empty-layer cleanup regressions are registered; native execution remains required. Region and manifest staging retain the original save-root traversal when a symlink is followed by a parent component. Complete and incremental session saves acquire shared ownership after job quiescence and snapshot capture, before destination validation, and retain it through payloads, metadata and dirty acknowledgement. Registered path and overlapping-session regressions include interrupted retries and incompatible clock refusal; native execution remains required.",
